WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:09.340 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Music]  i  absolutely love mountains ironically i  live in what's perhaps the flattest  country on god's green earth  i guess we crave what we don't have one  of my biggest passions in life is hiking  and exploring the rich landscapes the  planet has to offer  for me nothing beats sitting at a  campfire on a remote mountaintop  and gazing up at the stars after a long  day of intense walking and climbing  only to wake up the next day to find  your tent being ravaged by a group of  angry german cows  i try to plan at least one or two hiking  trips abroad annually  if it fits my work schedule and budget  and depending on the mercy of the dutch  weather god of  guess what it just might not rain today  i try to go on as many small day hikes  as i can right here at home  but in the meantime i have to stay  patient and wait for the next big trip  luckily in the meantime we have this  thing called video games which let you  live out your own fantasies and  adventures from the comfort of your own  home  now despite growing up in the early 90s  i never actually  experienced the zelda game up until  ocarina of time i vaguely remember  seeing one of my neighbors kids playing  a link to the past and thinking  this looks neat but i never played it  until way later  ocarina of time is the game that sparked  my love for the franchise and well  looking at the sales numbers i'm  guessing i wasn't the only one  as a kid i didn't exactly get hyped for  games they would simply magically appear  on my path  either from word of mouth making a  random pick at the video store or  playing it at a friend's house  you would think that when majira's mask  was announced the sequel to one of my  favorite games i would know about it as  soon as the word hit the streets but  actually no after i finished the game i  would simply set it aside  or replay it 30 more times and then go  out and play in the woods with my master  stick to slay imaginary monsters  i didn't follow any gaming news barely  read any magazines and the internet was  well  so by the time majira's mask released  instead of grabbing a sleeping bag and  camping in front of the store to get my  copy of the game at launch  my experience went a little more like  hey what do you got there  oh this it's a new zelda game remember  ocarina of time i think this is like the  sequel to it or something  oh cool let's play it i didn't even own  majira's mask on the n64 myself and  i don't think i ever did i would simply  go over to my cousin's house daily to  play it with him and after finishing it  together i borrowed it a couple of times  after and it grew out to become one of  my favorite games  it wasn't until my teenage years when i  first started getting hyped for games  long before they released  the first games to do so were mario  sunshine and the wind waker to a point  where i was constantly buying magazines  downloading trailer and gameplay footage  and at that age even waiting for half a  year for a game to finally drop felt  like an eternity  and then of course we had the twilight  princess reveal  need i say more so what does all this  nostalgic rambling have to do with  breath the wild  well up until my early to mid 20s i  enjoyed many different games and even  branched out to other platforms  i played games like half-life fallout  counter-strike silent hill  among many others but then something  happened it was like hitting a brick  wall  all of a sudden i stopped enjoying video  games  this was horrible timing for me as  skyward sword just came out  and trust me when i say i tried i really  really tried but i just couldn't get  through it  to this day i have never played past the  third dungeon myself  of course nowadays getting wrapped up in  zelda theories and editing videos i had  to do research and learn about the story  and lore found in the game  now this is by no means meant to be a  bash on skyward sword  i know there's plenty of people who  thoroughly enjoyed this game this  is purely a personal thing skyward sword  to me  represents the time when i started to  realize my enjoyment of video  games was starting to fade and this  wasn't unique to this game either  around this time i sold most of my games  and consoles something i regretted this  day by the way  and i rarely played any video games from  that point on i started focusing on  other stuff like  work and other hobbies as i couldn't  exactly force myself to like something i  clearly lost interest in  this state of mind persevered for a few  years and then 2014 came along  now obviously i didn't immediately go  back to being a full-blown gamer right  away but there was this tiny little  spark that ignited within me  and then it stayed mostly quiet for two  years but secretly i kept peeking into  breath the wild news now and again  i discovered the footage of the 2015  game awards presentation with anuma and  miyamoto and then of course we finally  got the first proper trailer in 2016  and with that that little spark grew  into a blaze that almost burned my  apartment down  as soon as i saw those gorgeous  landscapes climbing mechanics  cooking and general theme of survival  and non-linearity i knew that this  was gonna be a game changer to pass the  time between the trailer and the release  in 2017 i picked up fallout 4  which was already out for two years but  i never bothered to pick it up  and when breath of the wild finally came  out i did something i hadn't done  in many many years i took two weeks off  of work  closed myself off from society and  allowed myself to be totally immersed  into the world of hyrule  so what do i think of the game overall  does it have any flaws  of course it does no game is perfect and  it doesn't have to be  ocarina of time had flaws and to this  day it's regarded as one of the finest  achievements in gaming  as a matter of fact milestones usually  are flawed because they dive into  unknown territory  see when i play a game i don't play it  through the eyes of a critic  i can very easily shut that part of my  brain off and simply enjoy what is laid  out in front of me  instead of thinking wow my sword broke  already  man weapons are way too brittle this was  a poor decision made by nintendo  i just adapt to it and think to myself  okay weapons don't last very long  i'd better make sure to always have a  decent stock of different weapons on me  and just move on with my adventure  the part where i start analyzing the  game and picking apart its flaws and  shortcomings doesn't come until way  after i already completed the game  actually i would probably be the worst  critic in the world in that regard  if my job was to form an opinion write  an article and give a game a rating  right upon release  my reviews would mostly consist of very  generous scores  as you can probably deduce from my  channel one of my favorite things in the  zelda series are the dungeons  but instead of thinking the game is good  but it would have been much better if it  had  actual dungeons like the previous games  my reasoning would be  the game is amazing despite the fact  that it doesn't feature any real  dungeons  i could list the things i like or don't  like right here but this isn't meant to  be a review  no matter the flaws i can find and pick  apart about breath the wild  it doesn't take away that which i enjoy  most about the game  the fact that everyone has their own  unique adventure with it  it goes back to the old philosophy of  the first game on nes  where miyamoto wanted to incentivize  gamers to exchange  information tell their own stories of  things they've experienced and  discovered  and share strategies between each other  and now with the technological prowess  that is the internet this is easier than  ever  it is exactly what the series needed  the monumental success of ocarina of  time was both a blessing  and a curse for the franchise on one  hand it launched the series even further  into stardom  on the other it forced nintendo to stick  to a strict formula  now i don't want to step on any toes  here i have a huge soft spot for the  wind waker and i absolutely love  twilight princess  but can you really blame me when i say  that when you take a step  back and take a look at the full picture  most games released after ocarina of  time stayed in that little  comfort zone that ocarina of time  created and i can't blame nintendo for  it  the big exception amongst the 3d titles  released after ocarina of time was  majora's mask sure over the years it's  gained a much bigger appreciation and  went from a cult classic to a fan  favorite  nowadays it's no longer controversial to  say that majora's mask is your favorite  game  but trust me when i say that when it  first released there were a lot of  people who didn't like it  it was weird and confusing uncomfortable  and  what is happening this is reflected back  onto the sales numbers  you would think that a direct sequel to  one of the most popular and praised  games of all time would sell just as  well  if not better but this wasn't exactly  terminator 2 to the original  not even selling half as many copies as  its predecessor  this was a game that tried something  very different and initially it felt  like a slap in the face for a lot of the  fans of ocarina  people in general don't take change very  well unless it's given in small  increments  it's why politics is such a slow process  when a politician gets into office under  the name of  change we're not exactly expecting to  wake up the next day and everyone is  riding pandas to work  on the flip side as with anything else  in life if you repeat it again  and again too often it inevitably gets  stale by the time skyward sword came  along sales numbers had dwindled  significantly  you cannot just slap motion controls on  a game and expect it to feel like  something completely new  you are still doing the same stuff  except instead of pressing a button  you now have to flail your arms around  and again skyward sword isn't to blame  for this  it was the inevitable result of reusing  the same formula over and over again  and only adding small changes as not to  alienate the fanbase too much  every game following ocarina of time and  majora's mask still presented new ideas  but the core of the formula largely  stayed the same breath of the wild was  the game to finally break the mold and  change what we think a zelda game should  be  the series as a whole spans back so far  and it's well on its way to its fourth  decade just think about that for a  moment five years from now this  franchise will exist for 40 years  how many games do you know that are  still going strong today after so much  time  how many television shows or movie  franchises that started in the 80s are  still around today and haven't  disappeared or degraded in quality  this is a rare phenomenon and the way  nintendo managed to achieve this  is by having that one game that one  milestone that defines a generation  this isn't to say that the games in  between these milestones are bad  on the contrary they're all good and  your favorite game  doesn't have to be a milestone as i  stated before milestones are rarely  perfect  because they take bold new leaps into  unknown territory  it's much easier to copy something and  do it better than to try something  completely new  and innovative  when people say that breath the wild  isn't a masterpiece i  cannot argue against it in fact it would  be a pointless argument to have  as it's ultimately based on preference  to me personally  breath the wild is a masterpiece not  because its brush strokes are perfect or  its composition flawless  but because it dared to do something new  and executed well  i mean we're still talking about this  game experimenting with it and  discovering new things more than three  years after its release  i'm of the somewhat older generation of  zelda fans and it's so easy to forget  that there are so many young gamers out  there whose experience with the series  started with breath the wild in many  ways this is the ocarina of time of this  new generation  at the same time i've seen many people  my age who grew tired of the series  returning with a newfound enthusiasm  and because the game is so dynamic we  all have our own reasons for liking it  and our own stories to tell about the  adventures we had for those who did not  enjoy breath the wild  i completely understand big changes  means you're gonna draw in a new crowd  but you're also bound to lose some  the legend of zelda breath the wild  speaks to me because it reminds me of my  hiking trips  i know it's a cliche thing to say but  it's true out there  there's no drama no politics no internet  beef  just you and that mountaintop you want  to reach before nightfall  the game also reminded me that as a 32  year old man  i can still get totally immersed in a  fictional world and it single-handedly  reignited my passion for video games  to me this proves that childhood  nostalgia doesn't always have to dictate  your favorite experiences  and you're never too old to get into  something new modern internet culture  has granted levels of hype  never before seen and this devoted fan  base and all these great theorists  keeping up the hype with all these  creative theories and speculations and  storytelling  ensures that this franchise is stronger  than it's ever been  when we finally get new information  about the sequel we will undoubtedly be  in for a wild ride  and i hope you will be there with us  when it happens  [Music]  you

